Title: GRAILS
Author: the operator
Draft date: 2026-03-08
---

= Beat 01: THE FIND

EXT. SUBURBAN GARAGE SALE — RESEDA — DAY

White-hot San Fernando Valley sun. Folding tables loaded with junk. A hand-painted cardboard sign: EVERYTHING MUST GO.

TYLER (17) squints against the glare, poking through a folding table's underbelly. Ratty sneakers. AirPods in one ear. He's not looking for anything in particular.

His fingers hit cardboard. He pulls out a box, half-buried under a stack of National Geographics. He looks at it the way you look at something you don't understand.

He pays four dollars. He doesn't blink.

INT. TYLER'S CAR — CONTINUOUS

Tyler sits in the driver's seat with the box in his lap. His face is blank. He turns the box over once. Twice.

He props his phone against the steering wheel and hits record.

TYLER
(flat, mumbled)
Found this... lol.

He posts it. Barely watches the upload complete. Turns the key. Pulls out.

His phone ERUPTS — notification after notification cascading across the screen as he merges onto the street.

He doesn't look back at it.

INT. KEVIN'S APARTMENT — KOREATOWN — SAME TIME

KEVIN (30s, sharp eyes, never fully at rest) scrolls his feed in bed. He stops. Sits up. Watches Tyler's shaky video twice.

His expression doesn't change. But he's already reaching for his keys.

EXT. RESEDA STREETS — DAY

Kevin drives west on the 101 in traffic, one hand on the wheel, the other on his phone. He types a DM without looking at the keyboard.

He drives forty minutes and doesn't turn on music.

EXT. TYLER'S HOUSE — RESEDA — DAY

Kevin parks. Tyler comes out with the box. Money changes hands — cash, a small fold. Tyler barely counts it.

Kevin is already back in the car.

INT. KEVIN'S CAR — CONTINUOUS

Kevin holds the box on the passenger seat like it might shift on turns. He merges back onto the 101 north.

The city scrolls past. Strip malls. Overpasses. Palm trees cut against the smog line.

He takes an exit into the Valley.

EXT. STRIP MALL — VAN NUYS — DAY

The kind of strip mall that peaked in 1994 and has been coasting ever since. A RadioShack sign hangs by a single wire. A Spirit Halloween banner peels off a gutted department store. The parking lot is cracked asphalt with weeds pushing through.

Kevin pulls up. Looks at the storefront: ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE. Hand-lettered on the glass. A graded card in the window, backlit by a lamp inside.

He sits a beat. Then gets out with the box.


===

= Beat 02: THE SHOP

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — CONTINUOUS

The smell hits first: acid-free plastic, aged cardboard, the chalky ghost of forty-year-old bubblegum dust. The shop is layered, obsessive, dense with history.

Walls of graded slabs in acrylic cases. Binders of complete sets, spine-labeled by year. A framed Dominican Sugar Kings pennant. A pair of scuffed Jordans suspended in a vitrine mid-flight — frozen, eternal.

ZACH ZAKALOWICZ (40s) stands at the counter. Sun-faded 1984 Durham Bulls tee. A jeweler's loupe hanging from a leather cord around his neck. He has the posture of a man who is completely comfortable in exactly one place on earth.

He is currently occupied.

THREE GENERATIONS stand at the counter: GRANDFATHER (70s, windbreaker, the careful dignity of someone who has carried something a long way), FATHER (40s, tired eyes, a man who wanted this to work out), and BOY (10, watching everything with enormous attention).

Two shoeboxes of cards sit open on the counter. Zach works through them — carefully, honestly.

ZACH
(examining a card)
Bill Ripken error. '89 Fleer.
(sets it aside)
Your dad's got good instincts. This one's
real, it's funny, and it matters to people
who know.

The Father watches Zach's hands move through the cards.

ZACH (CONT'D)
Randy Johnson Marlboro ad — that's a
regional issue. Not worth what people think.

He separates cards into two piles with quiet authority.

ZACH (CONT'D)
Altogether... I'd say five hundred. Maybe
a little over. I want to be straight with you.

The Father nods slowly. The hope in his face adjusts — not gone, just recalibrated. He glances at the Grandfather.

Then Zach reaches under the counter.

He produces a slabbed card. PSA holder. Clean, flawless. He turns it so the light catches the label: 1989 UPPER DECK — KEN GRIFFEY JR. ROOKIE — GRADE 9.

He sets it on the counter, then turns it over. On the back of the slab, a holographic notation: RESTORED.

ZACH
I logged this one myself. Came in last
year in rough shape. I spent eleven hours
on it.
(beat)
The notation means I worked on it.
I won't pass it off as factory original.
Not to you. Not to anyone.

The Father stares at the card. Then at the notation.

The Grandfather says nothing. He reaches out with one finger and touches the edge of the slab.

The Father's eyes mist. He pulls his own father into a hug — sudden, quiet. The Grandfather holds on.

The Boy watches. He's trying to understand why adults are crying over cardboard. And then, slowly, he does: *time is inside the cardboard. And it's running out.*

Zach walks them to the door.

EXT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — CONTINUOUS

He holds the door. They file out into the Valley sun. The Father turns back.

FATHER
Thank you. For being straight with us.

Zach nods. He watches them go.

He is the last warm light in a dying strip mall. The parking lot spreads out before him — weeds, cracks, empty spaces. He stands there a moment, alone.

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — CONTINUOUS

He walks back to the counter. A RENT NOTICE sits in the corner, half-hidden under a binder. He's seen it. He doesn't move it.

Kevin is still in the chair in the back. He's been checking his phone. He looks up.

KEVIN
You done?

Zach looks at him. Looks at the box Kevin brought in.

He picks it up.

Everything slows.


===

= Beat 03: THE OFFER

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — CONTINUOUS

Zach holds the box the way a surgeon holds a newborn.

He carries it to his work table in the back. Sets it under the lamp. Pulls the loupe from around his neck and leans in.

Inside: wax packs, still sealed, still squared. A 1986 Fleer box set. Forty years old and sleeping.

His hands — the hands that have handled ten thousand cards — are trembling slightly.

He opens the loupe. Works methodically, scanning edges, surface texture, color registration. He holds a penlight at an oblique angle. He turns a pack over. Studies the wax seams.

Kevin watches from the chair. He's stopped checking his phone.

KEVIN
Well?

ZACH
(not looking up)
Give me a minute.

Another full minute. Maybe two. The shop is very quiet.

Zach straightens up. He sets the loupe on the table.

ZACH (CONT'D)
It's real.

Kevin exhales. Long.

ZACH (CONT'D)
The Jordan rookie. Bird-Magic XRC.
Patrick Ewing. Kobe in an unopened wax
from '86.
(beat)
This is... this is extraordinary.

KEVIN
Ten thousand. Upfront. Perfect restoration —
just like new. Gift for my brother.

ZACH
That's too much money for a gift for
your brother.

Kevin smiles. It's not an unkind smile.

KEVIN
There's a man who wants to meet you.

ZACH
What kind of man?

KEVIN
The kind who's already decided.

Beat.

ZACH
Decided what?

KEVIN
That you're the right person for a job.

Zach looks at the box. Looks at Kevin. Looks at the rent notice on the counter.

ZACH
What kind of job?

Kevin doesn't answer. He just stands up.

KEVIN
Koreatown. Tonight. Someone will pick you up.

He leaves. The bell over the door rings once and goes quiet.

Zach stands alone with the box under the lamp. He puts his loupe back around his neck.

He already knows he's going to go. He just hasn't said it yet.

EXT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — NIGHT

A car idles at the curb. Zach locks the shop. He stands a moment, looking at the hand-lettered sign in the window.

He gets in the car.

EXT. KOREATOWN ROOFTOP — DUSK

Los Angeles at that hour when the smog turns gold and the city looks like it was made for movies. A rooftop with folding chairs and a low table. The view is the whole basin, orange and infinite.

JAE-WOOK (40s, the kind of calm that only comes from having made difficult choices before) pours soju into two small glasses. He does not hurry.

He is not who the movies told you he would be.

DEJA (late 20s, phone in hand, seated slightly apart) takes notes without looking like she's taking notes. She has the smile of someone who is always three moves ahead and doesn't need you to know it.

Jae-Wook slides a glass toward Zach.

JAE-WOOK
One hundred thousand dollars.

Zach doesn't touch the glass.

ZACH
For what?

JAE-WOOK
For what you do. Applied elsewhere.

ZACH
I need to know—

JAE-WOOK
(quietly)
You need to drink your soju and listen.

A beat. Zach picks up the glass.

The city burns orange below them.


===

= Beat 04: THE EX

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — MORNING

The shop opens. Zach flips the sign. He's running on not enough sleep and too much to think about.

He's behind the counter sorting binders when the bell over the door rings.

He doesn't look up.

ZACH
Be right with you.

Silence. The particular quality of someone standing very still.

He looks up.

ELIZABETH (40s) — Zab to everyone who knows her — stands in the doorway. She is dressed with the precision of someone who has learned to project competence as armor. Sophisticated. Quietly exhausted. The kind of beautiful that comes from having been somewhere difficult and come back.

She holds herself carefully. Like she's deciding something.

ZACH
(quietly)
Zab.

ELIZABETH
Hi.

She lets the door close behind her. She moves into the shop the way you move into a place you used to live — noting what's changed, noting what hasn't.

ELIZABETH (CONT'D)
I came about some business. There's
an authentication project—

ZACH
Sure.

ELIZABETH
—that requires a specialist familiar
with pre-certification era provenance.

ZACH
Sure.

She stops. He's not looking at her the way a person looks at a business contact.

ELIZABETH
Are you going to make this hard?

ZACH
I'm being agreeable.

ELIZABETH
You're being agreeable the way a wall
is agreeable.

She moves along the shelves, running a finger along the edge of a binder. Not looking at him. Looking at everything that's his.

ELIZABETH (CONT'D)
The shop looks the same.

ZACH
Mostly.

ELIZABETH
New vitrine for the Jordans.

ZACH
Two years ago.

She stops at the vitrine. Looks at the shoes — mid-flight, frozen.

Her hand goes to her jacket pocket. A small, unconscious gesture. He sees it. He knows what's in that pocket. He doesn't say anything.

ELIZABETH
I should have called first.

ZACH
Probably.

ELIZABETH
I'm with Togg now. As of three months ago.
VP of Authenticity for his platform.

The word sits in the room.

ZACH
Congratulations.

ELIZABETH
It's a significant position.

ZACH
I'm sure it is.

ELIZABETH
(quietly)
It pays well, Zach.

He looks at her. She looks back. They've had this argument before, in different rooms, different years.

ELIZABETH (CONT'D)
That's not — I didn't mean it like that.

ZACH
I know.

A beat. She turns toward the door.

ELIZABETH
I'll send the project brief by email.

ZACH
Okay.

ELIZABETH
It was good to see the shop.

She's almost out the door.

ZACH
Zab.

She stops.

ZACH (CONT'D)
What you meant to say.

She doesn't turn around. A long moment.

ELIZABETH
I came to see if it was still here. All of it.

She leaves.

The bell rings once and goes quiet.

Zach stands behind the counter. He reaches up and touches the loupe around his neck.


===

= Beat 05: The Mark

INT. KOREATOWN — BACK OFFICE — NIGHT

A private room above a restaurant. The sounds of the street below — distant, warm. Jae-Wook sits across from Zach. Between them: a laptop, open. On screen: a YouTube channel.

GREGG TOGG stares back from the thumbnail, grinning at 8K resolution, holding a rookie card in latex gloves under god-ray lighting. His teeth are very white.

Jae-Wook turns the laptop to face Zach.

JAE-WOOK
Gregg Togg.

ZACH
I know who he is.

JAE-WOOK
Then you know what he does.

ZACH
He buys collections. He films it. He has
forty million subscribers who watch him open
packs like it's a religious experience.

JAE-WOOK
What else?

Zach looks at the screen. Studies it.

ZACH
He uses the word "authentic" the way a
company uses it in a tagline.

JAE-WOOK
(a small nod)
He is about to launch something. A memecoin.
"$GOAT." Sports memorabilia, tokenized.
Backed by his verified collection.

Deja opens a folder of documents, slides them across.

DEJA
He's been building the anchor asset for
six months. A multimillion-dollar verified
collection — the crown jewel is a 1986
Fleer sealed box. Jordan rookie inside.
Kobe. The full grail stack.

ZACH
He doesn't have that box.

JAE-WOOK
Not yet.

ZACH
Where's he getting it?

Jae-Wook holds Zach's gaze.

JAE-WOOK
That's our problem to solve. Before he does.

Zach sits back.

ZACH
You want me to build a forgery.

The word settles.

JAE-WOOK
I want you to build the box. The way you
would build any restoration — with precision,
with care, with your full attention.
(beat)
The only difference is the provenance.

ZACH
The only difference is it's a crime.

JAE-WOOK
Yes.

ZACH
What happens to the real box?

JAE-WOOK
It comes to us first. We swap them.
He launches the coin. The market goes
stratospheric. We are short.

Deja, without looking up from her phone:

DEJA
The positions are already being built.
We're waiting on you.

Zach looks at the laptop. Togg on the screen, mid-laugh, holding the card.

ZACH
He treats everything as a collectible.
The crowd. The people around him.

JAE-WOOK
Yes.

ZACH
He doesn't actually love any of it.

JAE-WOOK
That is why we don't feel badly.
(beat)
Do you?

Zach looks at the screen a moment longer.

Then he looks at the door. And the rent notice he's carrying in his jacket pocket.

ZACH
Tell me everything about the collection.
The print run. The wax stock. The era.
Every detail you have.

Jae-Wook nods once. He refills their glasses.

The planning begins.


===

= Beat 06: THE CREW

INT. KOREATOWN — KARAOKE BAR — NIGHT

A private room. Six screens cycling visualizers. The song is something enormous and melodramatic. Nobody is singing.

DEJA sits with her laptop at the table, a spreadsheet visible on screen — positions, targets, timeline columns. She looks like she's planning a birthday party. She is not planning a birthday party.

JAE-WOOK stands by the door, steady as furniture.

HYE-JIN (late 20s, rave scene in her blood, OnlyFans-visible but somehow simultaneously everywhere and nowhere) leans in the corner with her phone, watching a livestream of one of Togg's recent events.

Zach sits with a notepad. He's making a list of materials. He keeps crossing things out and rewriting them.

JAE-WOOK
Deja.

DEJA
(without looking up)
The $GOAT launch is in eight weeks. The
anchor buy — his people trying to acquire the
box — we think it moves in the next three.
We need the forgery complete before then.

ZACH
Complete means perfect. Perfect means
I need the real box in hand for at
least a day. Side by side.

DEJA
We're working on that.

ZACH
"Working on it" isn't a timeline.

DEJA
(still not looking up)
Neither is your notepad.

Jae-Wook to Hye-Jin:

JAE-WOOK
Tell him.

HYE-JIN
(still watching the stream)
Togg's holding a showcase event in three weeks.
Hills Panopticon. Invitation only — but
his team has been looking for influencer
energy. Micro talent, real-feel aesthetic.
(beat)
I'm going.

ZACH
Going as what?

HYE-JIN
Myself. That's the whole thing. I move
through Togg's retinue looking exactly like
what I am. People find me. I don't find them.

ZACH
And inside — you're looking for?

HYE-JIN
Access. The collection layout. Where
the real box lives. Whether the security
is theater or actual.

DEJA
And how long his attention span is,
which from all evidence is short.

Hye-Jin smiles.

HYE-JIN
He'll be very interested in me for about
a week and a half. Then I'll be in the right
rooms for the rest of the time before he forgets
to revoke my access.

Zach looks around the table. These are the people he's trusting.

ZACH
Why does Jae-Wook do this? Why not just —
he clearly has resources. Why this?

Silence. Jae-Wook looks at him.

JAE-WOOK
Because Togg's platform laundered a
collection that belonged to a man who is
dead. He authenticated it, securitized it,
sold it as verified — and the family got nothing.
(beat)
I'm principled about what I steal.

Deja's smile stays exactly where it is.

ZACH
And me? Why am I the right person?

JAE-WOOK
Because you're the only one who will
build it the way the real one was made.
With love.
(beat)
You can't fake that. But you can apply it.

A long beat. Zach looks at his notepad.

ZACH
I need period-correct wax stock.
Correct-era cardboard. Pigment matched
to a 1986 press run within a spectrophotometer's
margin. That's not stuff you find on eBay.

JAE-WOOK
Tell me what you need. We will find it.

ZACH
Give me two days to write the full list.

He goes back to the notepad. Crosses something out. Rewrites it.

The song changes to something slow and enormous.


===

= Beat 07: PROCUREMENT

INT./EXT. VARIOUS LOCATIONS — LOS ANGELES — DAY/NIGHT

A MONTAGE — but not of speed. Of obsession.

---

INT. PRINTING ARCHIVE — GLENDALE — DAY

Zach stands in the stacks of a commercial printing archive, running his fingers along the edge of a paper sample. He holds it to the light. Shakes his head.

ARCHIVIST
It's the closest I have to the '86 run.

ZACH
It's off. The grain direction.
(sets it down)
I need to see the California supplier records
from '84 to '87. Cardboard stock only.

The Archivist stares at him.

ARCHIVIST
Nobody asks for that.

ZACH
I'm asking.

---

INT. COLLECTOR'S GARAGE — BURBANK — DAY

A man (COLLECTOR, 60s, suspicious, proprietary) opens a flat of sealed wax packs and lets Zach hold one. Zach sniffs it. Holds it under a UV lamp.

COLLECTOR
You're not gonna open it?

ZACH
No. I need to understand the wax itself.
The composition. The feel.

The Collector watches Zach handle the pack the way a man handles his own child.

COLLECTOR
You restore cards for people like Togg?

ZACH
I restore cards.

He sets the pack down carefully. He buys the lot with cash from an envelope.

---

EXT. FLEA MARKET — LONG BEACH — MORNING

Zach moves through a long row of vendors. Finds an old-stock printing supply table. Digs through flat boxes of obsolete pigment cartridges — 1980s overstock.

He holds a cartridge up to his loupe. Checks the date. Checks it again. Something fires in his eyes.

He buys the whole box of obsolete ink. The vendor is confused and grateful.

---

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — BACK ROOM — NIGHT

The materials spread across the work table. Zach lays them out with the precision of a surgeon prepping an OR. He photographs each element. Documents them. Begins a notebook.

On one page, in precise handwriting: a spectrophotometer reading of a 1986 Fleer pack surface. He cross-references it against a sample of his acquired pigment.

The margin: 0.003. Within tolerance.

He allows himself a small exhale.

Then he begins.

---

EXT. TOGG EVENT SPACE — THE HILLS — DAY

Procurement errand. Zach moves through the crowd of a Togg industry showcase — press and influencers milling around overlit glass cases. He has a specific reason to be here. He tells himself this.

Across the room, under the god-ray lighting:

ELIZABETH.

She's in her element — moving through the event with practiced ease, speaking quietly to a handler, gesturing at a display case. She looks like she belongs. She looks like she's had to work at looking like she belongs.

Their eyes meet.

Neither moves. A held beat — the room between them is full of glass cases and backlit cards and the white noise of two hundred people performing enthusiasm.

She crosses to him first.

ELIZABETH
You're not here for the event.

ZACH
Neither are you.

ELIZABETH
I work here. You don't.

ZACH
Procurement errand.

ELIZABETH
(a look)
Whose?

ZACH
Client.

A beat. Her hand finds her jacket pocket. Unconscious. He sees it.

ELIZABETH
It's good work, actually. The collection.
If it's what they say it is.

ZACH
Is it?

She looks at him carefully.

ELIZABETH
I'm still running the verification.

ZACH
Let me know what you find.

He moves away. She watches him go.

Her hand stays in her pocket a moment longer.


===

= Beat 08: THE PRIVATE DINNER

INT. PRIVATE DINING ROOM — WEST HOLLYWOOD — NIGHT

White tablecloths. Candlelight engineered to look effortless. A room that doesn't have a name because the people who eat here don't need it to.

Jae-Wook is already seated when Zach arrives. Across from him: HENRI BEAUMONT-LACROIX (60s, Geneva accent, eyes that have priced everything they've ever looked at). He wears a watch that costs more than the shop.

Two SAUDI REPRESENTATIVES sit adjacent — attentive, formal, reading nothing in their expressions.

Zach sits. He is very aware of his shoes.

JAE-WOOK
This is the craftsman I mentioned.

Henri looks at Zach the way a jeweler looks at a stone — not unkindly, but commercially.

HENRI
Your work. I've seen examples.
(a pause)
The Ripken restoration. The '52 Topps set
you brought back from flood damage.

ZACH
You've done your research.

HENRI
Always.

The WAITER pours wine. Henri swirls without looking.

HENRI (CONT'D)
The item, when complete. If authenticated —
and I understand it will be, convincingly —
moves to a private buyer in the Gulf.
The number has multiple commas.

ZACH
What number?

Henri tells him. Quietly. The way you say a number in a room like this.

Zach keeps his face still. He's good at keeping his face still.

HENRI (CONT'D)
The buyer is not interested in the
memecoin economy. He is interested in
the object as object. Its beauty.
Its history. What it means to hold
a thing that has outlasted the world
that made it.

A pause. Zach looks at the man.

ZACH
He doesn't know it's a forgery.

HENRI
He is buying provenance. We are providing it.

JAE-WOOK
The public crash is separate. The
buyer takes his purchase before the
market event. He is unharmed.

ZACH
Is that true?

JAE-WOOK
(quietly)
I don't lie about mathematics.

Zach sits with this. The wine is very good. He doesn't say so.

ZACH
And what about what he's actually buying?

HENRI
He is buying an exceptional object.
Made by exceptional hands.
(looks at Zach's hands on the table)
Your hands.
(a beat)
What is authenticity, ultimately,
if not belief?

ZACH
It's the truth.

HENRI
The truth is a provenance document and
a spectrophotometer reading.
(small smile)
Both of which will be impeccable.

Zach looks at Jae-Wook. Jae-Wook meets his eyes. He doesn't flinch, and he doesn't perform.

INT. UBER — WEST HOLLYWOOD — NIGHT

Zach in the backseat. The city scrolls past — lights, motion, the basin in all directions. He sits very still.

He's past the last exit. He knows it. He's known it since the rooftop.

He puts his hand on the loupe around his neck and holds it.


===

= Beat 09: THE LAUNCH

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — NIGHT

Zach is mid-work. Loupe. Penlight. Tweezers. The amber light of his single work lamp over the table. The shop is dark everywhere else.

His phone buzzes. A text from Deja: a link and a single word — *now.*

He opens it. Casts to the small TV he keeps in the corner.

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — CONTINUOUS

The screen fills with GREGG TOGG — studio lighting at its most god-ray-maximum, sitting in a custom chair in his Hills Panopticon, grinning at forty million simultaneous viewers.

TOGG
(on screen, amplified certainty)
Sports memorabilia is the last real asset class.
You know why? Because a rookie card is a
rookie card. A '86 Fleer Jordan is a
'86 Fleer Jordan. It doesn't dilute.
It doesn't inflate. It *authenticates.*

Crowd sounds. A live ticker at the bottom of the screen — $GOAT predictions already moving.

TOGG (CONT'D)
(on screen)
I've been sitting on this for six months.
The anchor asset for $GOAT. The crown jewel
of a verified, authenticated, insured
eight-figure collection—

He holds up a box. Similar to Zach's. Not identical. A placeholder — the real acquisition hasn't happened yet. Togg doesn't care about the gap between the announcement and the thing.

TOGG (CONT'D)
(on screen)
The 1986 Fleer sealed box. Michael Jordan
rookie. Larry Bird and Magic. Kobe.
This is what backs the coin. This is
the grail. And it's ours.

The market ticker at the bottom SPIKES.

DEJA (O.S.)
(on phone, just connected)
You watching?

ZACH
I'm watching.

DEJA (O.S.)
Prediction markets already moving.
Pre-launch price just doubled.

ZACH
He doesn't have the box.

DEJA (O.S.)
He announced the acquisition. In Togg's
world, that's the same thing.

Zach watches the ticker move.

ZACH
How long do we have?

DEJA (O.S.)
His people make the buy in two weeks.
If we don't have the swap ready before then—

ZACH
I know.

He ends the call. Looks at the screen. Togg is still talking — confident, complete, a man who has never doubted that the world would agree with whatever he announced.

Zach looks at the work on his table. The forgery in progress.

He puts his phone face-down and goes back to work.

EXT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — SAME TIME

A car parked across the lot. Engine off. Two silhouettes inside, watching the shop's single lit window.

One of them writes something in a notebook.


===

= Beat 10: THE CROSSING

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — BACK ROOM — NIGHT

The forgery is nearly complete. Under the lamp: a 1986 Fleer box, every surface period-correct, every wax seal within spectrophotometer tolerance. It looks like something that has been waiting forty years to be opened.

It looks exactly like the real thing. Because it is Zach's real thing — the most technically precise work of his entire career, built with the same obsessive care he has given every authentic restoration. This is the revelation taking shape in his hands.

He sets down the tweezers. Sits back. Looks at what he's made.

The bell over the shop door rings.

He doesn't move. It's late. The shop is closed.

Footsteps on the floor. Jae-Wook appears in the doorway of the back room. He looks at the work on the table.

A long silence.

JAE-WOOK
May I?

ZACH
Don't touch it. The wax is still setting.

Jae-Wook leans in. Studies it without touching. His face is still.

JAE-WOOK
It's perfect.

ZACH
Not yet. Another day.

Jae-Wook straightens. Looks at Zach.

JAE-WOOK
I came to ask you something.

ZACH
Ask.

JAE-WOOK
Do you understand what you're crossing?

The question sits in the room. Zach looks at his hands.

ZACH
I'm a man who built his whole life
on the difference between a thing and
a copy of a thing. I've written that line
in my own ledger for twenty years.

JAE-WOOK
And now you're crossing it.

ZACH
(looks up)
Yes.

JAE-WOOK
Do you know why you can do it?
The craft, I mean. Why it comes
naturally to you.

Zach looks at the box.

ZACH
Because I've spent twenty years
understanding the real thing. You can't
make a perfect forgery if you don't
love the original.

He reaches out and adjusts one edge of the box — a micro-correction, imperceptible to anyone else.

ZACH (CONT'D)
The line is thinner than I thought.
Between preservation and fabrication.
(beat)
I'm not sure what that means about me.

JAE-WOOK
It means you're a craftsman.
The world decides what to do with
craftsmen. We just do the work.

He looks at the box once more. He doesn't say it's beautiful, but something in his face says it.

JAE-WOOK (CONT'D)
Tomorrow night. We move.

He leaves. His footsteps cross the shop floor. The bell rings.

Zach sits alone with the box under the amber lamp.

He is not sure he's the same person who walked into that rooftop meeting. He's not sure the old version of him would recognize what he just built.

He's not sure he minds.

He covers the box carefully with acid-free cloth. Turns off the lamp.

The shop goes dark.


===

= Beat 11: THE LOSS

INT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — DAY

Zach is at the counter. He's been watching the car across the lot — it's been there three days. Same car. Different occupants, but same posture. The way people sit when they're watching.

He saw the same pair of eyes twice on different streets last week.

He goes about his work. Grading a collection. Writing a condition report. His hands are steady. His eyes keep moving to the window.

His phone rings. Unknown number.

ZACH
Heritage House.

VOICE (ON PHONE)
Mr. Zakalowicz. This is the property
management office for the Van Nuys
Commercial Center.

A beat.

ZACH
I know who you are.

VOICE (ON PHONE)
I'm calling to advise you that per section
12.4 of your lease agreement, the landlord
is exercising the early termination option
due to outstanding rent arrears.

ZACH
I've been making payments.

VOICE (ON PHONE)
The arrears predate the current payment
schedule. The option has always been
available to exercise.
(a beat)
We need the space vacated in thirty days.

The line sits there.

ZACH
Thirty days.

VOICE (ON PHONE)
We're sorry for the disruption, Mr. Zakalowicz.

He hangs up.

EXT. ZZ HERITAGE HOUSE — DUSK

Zach stands in the parking lot. Boxes around him — he's already started moving things. The cracked asphalt. The weeds. The strip mall dying in the last light of the San Fernando Valley.

He's holding the padded case with the forgery inside. Finished. Complete. The most technically precise object he's ever made.

He doesn't have a shop anymore.

He stands very still.

Then his phone rings. Deja.

DEJA (ON PHONE)
Did you see what Togg posted?

INT./EXT. — ZACH ON PHONE — CONTINUOUS

He watches the video on his phone.

On screen: Togg on livestream. Behind him, barely visible in the set dressing — Elizabeth. Her nameplate is new: VP OF AUTHENTICITY.

TOGG (ON PHONE SCREEN)
(expansive, generous)
I want to introduce someone who's going
to make $GOAT the most credible launch
in this space. Elizabeth Chen — the world's
foremost AI authentication specialist —
is the new VP of Authenticity
for the Togg Collection.

Applause. Togg beaming. Elizabeth smiling for the camera — precise, composed, professional.

Zach watches. He sees her hand go to her hip — that unconscious reach for her jacket pocket. She stops it halfway. Folds her hands instead.

He turns off the screen.

DEJA (ON PHONE)
Zach. This doesn't change anything.
The authentication system is what we
planned around. We still know its parameters.

ZACH
I know.

DEJA (ON PHONE)
Does it complicate things for you
personally?

Long pause.

ZACH
No.

He looks at the shop. The hand-lettered sign. The dark window where the graded Jordans used to glow.

ZACH (CONT'D)
I'll be at the meet tonight.

He ends the call.

He stands there a moment longer. Then he picks up the case and walks to his car.

The shop is dark behind him.


===

= Beat 12: FINAL COORDINATION

INT. KOREATOWN BACKROOM — NIGHT

A room above a restaurant. The same table as before, but the mood is different — focused, compressed, the energy of a thing that is actually happening.

Zach. Jae-Wook. Deja. Hye-Jin. A map of Togg's property on the table, annotated in Deja's precise handwriting.

Hye-Jin has been inside Togg's world for three weeks. She looks slightly different — harder at the edges, more fluent in a language she doesn't love.

HYE-JIN
The security is mostly theater. The
main event space runs two guards on
the collection room — they rotate
every ninety minutes. The third rotation
has a gap.

She points to a mark on the map.

HYE-JIN (CONT'D)
Display table is here. The real box will
be in a glass case, sealed. The case is
Togg's own design — he had it custom built
for the launch. The lock is a keypad.

DEJA
We have the code?

HYE-JIN
We will tomorrow. His assistant
changes it every week, but she
uses a pattern.

DEJA
(writing)
I'll confirm by noon.

ZACH
The swap itself takes under four minutes
if the case is open and the crowd is
where you say it'll be.

JAE-WOOK
Where will the crowd be?

HYE-JIN
Wherever I am.
(a beat, not quite smiling)
I've been useful to them.
I've been very consistent
about being exactly what they expected.

A short silence. Deja looks at her.

DEJA
You okay?

HYE-JIN
Ask me after.

Deja nods. She looks back at the map.

DEJA
The coin launch goes live at 9 PM.
Authentication window is 8 to 8:45 —
that's when Elizabeth runs the AI rig
over the collection. The box has to
be in the case before 8.

ZACH
The swap happens during the reception.
6 to 7:30. Before authentication.

JAE-WOOK
The short positions trigger automatically
when $GOAT hits the target peak.
Deja has the numbers.

DEJA
Fifteen percent above launch price.
That's when the positions pay.

JAE-WOOK
Everyone walks away with exactly what
was agreed. No more, no less.

He looks around the table. Each person.

JAE-WOOK (CONT'D)
If something goes wrong — if the swap
is compromised, if Zach's cover breaks,
if Hye-Jin is exposed — everyone
disperses. We do not improvise.

HYE-JIN
And the forgery?

JAE-WOOK
Goes with Zach. No evidence.

ZACH
What if Elizabeth—

JAE-WOOK
She runs the authentication. She
confirms the box. That's her job.
Her AI system is not looking for this.

Zach doesn't say anything. He looks at the map.

Deja watches him.

DEJA
(quietly, just to him)
Her system authenticates based on the
parameters she trained it on.
If the forgery is within those parameters—

ZACH
It is.

DEJA
Then she won't see it.

Zach nods. He picks up the padded case from the floor beside him. Sets it on the table.

Everyone looks at it.

ZACH
It's ready.


===

= Beat 13: THE SWAP

INT. TOGG HILLS PANOPTICON — RECEPTION — EVENING

Togg's penthouse has been opened into a collector's showcase — glass cases under god-ray lighting, everything immaculate, everything a backdrop. Eight-figure items glowing under custom beams. TWO HUNDRED PEOPLE performing enthusiasm.

The room is loud in the way expensive rooms are loud — the sound of people who are very good at being seen.

ZACH moves through it in a sport coat, a lanyard that says APPRAISER, a demeanor of professional disinterest. He's been in rooms like this enough times to know how to disappear in one.

He has the padded case in a document bag.

Across the room: HYE-JIN, holding court near the bar. Six people in her orbit. She doesn't look at Zach. She doesn't need to.

INT. TOGG HILLS PANOPTICON — COLLECTION ROOM — EVENING

The inner room. Glass cases. Quieter. Two GUARDS at the entrance rotate every ninety minutes.

The real box is in the center case — sealed, lit, the crown jewel on display.

Zach works the room perimeter. Studying cases. Making notes on a clipboard. This is what appraisers do.

His phone vibrates once. Deja's signal: rotation started. Ninety-minute window open.

He moves to the center case. The keypad. He types the code Deja confirmed.

The case opens.

His hands are steady. They're always steady. Twenty years of handling things that can't be fumbled.

He sets the real box in the document bag. He places his forgery in the case. He closes it. He locks it.

Fifty-eight seconds.

He walks back into the main reception.

INT. TOGG HILLS PANOPTICON — RECEPTION — CONTINUOUS

Hye-Jin's group has migrated toward the center of the room. Natural drift. The guards are exactly where they're supposed to be.

Zach passes near Hye-Jin. He doesn't look at her.

From behind him, her voice, casual, aimed elsewhere:

HYE-JIN
(to her orbit)
—the thing about Fleer is the printing.
They were a bubblegum company.
They didn't know what they were making.

PERSON IN ORBIT
Is that why they're worth so much?

HYE-JIN
It's part of it. The other part is
that someone put it in a box and
kept it safe for forty years.

She finally looks at Zach, just for a moment. Just long enough.

He holds her gaze for half a second. Then walks on.

EXT. TOGG HILLS PANOPTICON — PARKING STRUCTURE — EVENING

Zach emerges into the cool air of the parking structure. He can see the event venue across the canyon — all the lights.

He finds Deja's car. Gets in.

She's already running the short positions on a laptop.

DEJA
(without looking up)
Clean?

ZACH
Clean.

She nods once. Types something.

DEJA
The box?

He hands her the document bag. She opens it. Looks at the real box. She doesn't touch it — she just looks at it, for a long beat.

DEJA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
It's beautiful.

ZACH
Yes.

They sit in the parking structure as the lights of the event burn across the canyon.

DEJA
Now we wait for Elizabeth to do her job.


===

= Beat 14: THE AUTHENTICATION

INT. TOGG HILLS PANOPTICON — COLLECTION ROOM — NIGHT

The room has been cleared for the main event. The two hundred people are outside — held back by velvet and anticipation. Through the glass: Togg working the crowd, live on stream.

ELIZABETH stands alone at the center case with the authentication rig. A compact AI system — her design, her training data, her parameters. She's done this a hundred times.

She positions the optical scanner over the box. The system initializes.

On the monitors outside: Togg narrating to forty million viewers.

TOGG (ON MONITOR)
This is the moment. The verification
that backs everything. The AI system
that makes $GOAT the most credible
launch in the space.

The authentication system runs. Elizabeth watches the readouts.

Outside in the crowd, DEJA watches on a laptop. Her positions are loaded.

In the parking structure: Zach and Deja's car is gone. He's already clear.

The readout completes. Elizabeth studies it.

The Jordan card: authenticated. Stratospheric. The system assigns it a confidence score so high it's almost theoretical.

$GOAT HITS ITS LAUNCH PRICE. Then climbs. Then climbs faster.

On Deja's laptop, the short position triggers.

DEJA (V.O.)
(into earpiece, barely audible)
Positions pay.

Elizabeth is still looking at the readout.

She reads it again.

The authentication is clean. Her AI confirmed it. Her system. Her work.

She straightens. Looks at the box through the glass.

Her hand moves to her jacket pocket.

The loupe is there. Small. Old. Matching the one Zach still wears around his neck.

She looks at the box.

She knows this kind of work. She has spent years training a system to see it. But the system sees spectrophotometer tolerances and provenance documents. Not hands.

She presses the loupe to her eye.

She looks at the box.

For a very long time.


===

= Beat 15: RESOLUTION

INT. TOGG HILLS PANOPTICON — COLLECTION ROOM — NIGHT

Elizabeth lowers the loupe.

She looks at the readout one more time. The authentication is clean. Her AI said so.

She signs the certificate.

EXT. PARKING STRUCTURE — SAME TIME

Deja's laptop: $GOAT peaks. The positions pay — fully, cleanly, exactly as calculated. She closes the screen.

DEJA
(into earpiece)
Done.

INT. TOGG HILLS PANOPTICON — MAIN STAGE — NIGHT

Togg holds the certificate up to the camera. Forty million people watch.

TOGG
Authenticated. Verified. The grail stack
is real, it is ours, and this is what
backs the coin. This is what changes
the market.

Applause. The ticker climbing.

EXT./INT. VARIOUS — NIGHT

Jae-Wook, at a quiet table somewhere in Koreatown, receives a text. He reads it. Folds his phone. Pours one more glass.

Hye-Jin, outside on the event terrace, looks at her phone. She exhales once. She does not let anyone see it.

Deja, in the parking structure, closes the laptop and sits very still for a moment. She has been planning this for fourteen months. She lets it land.

INT. MOTEL ROOM — VAN NUYS — NIGHT

Zach sits on the edge of the bed. The document bag with the real box is in the safe in the closet.

He turns on the TV. Togg's celebration is still streaming — the ticker still climbing, the crowd still performing enthusiasm.

He watches it for a while.

Then he turns it off.

He reaches up and touches the loupe around his neck.

He sits in the quiet.

He doesn't know yet that Elizabeth saw something. He doesn't know yet what she'll do with it.

He doesn't know yet that the car that was parked outside his shop for three days belonged to a detective named SOLIS who has been building a case for six weeks.

But right now, in this room, the box is real and the job is done and he is still the man who built the most precise thing he's ever made, with love, in the amber light of a shop that no longer exists.

He opens his notebook. He writes:

                         ZACH (V.O.)
          The line between preservation and fabrication
          is thinner than I thought.

          I used to believe that was a warning.

He closes the notebook.

FADE TO BLACK.


===

